################################################
    "Chapter 1: A Tour of Computer Systems"
################################################

  - C programs are stored as an "Executable Object Program", or "Executable Object Files"
  - four phases to the compilation system:
    * Preprocessor
      - Reads the system header files and inserts them directly in, as well as directives
      - Generally turns into a .i file (hello.i)
    * Compiler
      - Compiles hello.i into a .s file (hello.s)
    * Assembler
      - Turns hello.s into machine-language, called a "Relocatable Object Program", stored in a .o file (hello.o)
    * Linker
      - Merges the functions from other .h files into our program. creates the hello file (no extension), the final program!

  - Buses carry information in fixed-sized chunks of bytes known as "Words". I've found Word size is *generally* the same as the bitrate, but not guaranteed.
    Note: You can check your word size with WORD_SIZE in some of the /usr/include/<example,h> files.
  - Devices connected to I/O buses via a controller or an adapter:
    * Controller: Chip sets in the device itself, or on the system's motherboard
    * Adapter: card that pugs into a slot on the mobo, generally pci-e

  "Main Memory"
  - temporary storage device that holds both a program and the data it manipulates; generally a collection of DRAM chips.
  - Organized as linear arraya of bytes, each with an array index, starting from zero.

  "CPU"
  - Executes instructions stored in main memory.
  - "Register": Word-size storage device in the CPU known as the "Program Counter (PC)".
  - At any given time, PC points at machine-langauge instruction in main memory
  - Executes instruction pointed at by the PC, then updates the PC to the next instruction
  - "Register File": Storage device containing the full list of word-size registers, each with a unique name.
  - "Arithmetic/Logic Unit (ALU)": computres new data and address values
    * Simple instructions include Load, Store, Operate, Jump
  - The processsor may take millions of times longer to read data from a hard disk than from memory
  - The CPU can read data from the register up to 100x faster than memory(!) This "Processor-Memory Gap" continues to grow, as making CPUs faster is easier than making memory faster
  - "cache": temporary staging areas for info that the processor will likely need in the future
    * L1 can be accessed almost as quickly as the register file; L2 is 5-10x slower, but still 5-10x faster than ram
    * The newest hardware has L3 caches that can get quite large (64mb on AMD 9950X)
    * Caches are created with "Static Random Access Memory (SRAM)"
  - "Application Programmers who are aware of cache memories can exploit them to improve the performance of their programs by an order of magnitude"

  "Processes"
  - a "process" is an OS abstraction for a running program
  - "concurrency": when the instruction of one process are interleaved with the instructions of another
  - "Context switching" allows even a single-core CPU to handle multiple programs at once
    * the context, aka the state info the process needs to run, is all saved, the context of the new process is restored, and vice versa
  - the "kernel" is the portion of OS code that is always persistent in memory
  - when application programs start or read/write a file, the kernel executes a special "system call" instruction, giving the kernel control

  "Threads"
  - "threads" are multiple execution units, each running in the context of the process and sharing the same code and data
  - Used for concurrency, and by multi-core processors (which can generally handle twice as many threads as they have cores, via hyperthreading!)

  "Virtual Memory"
  - Provides every process with the illusion that is has exclusive use of the main memory
  - This is called that program's "virtual address space" (listed in order of address number:
    * "Program Code and Data:" code starts at the same fixed address for all processes, followed by data locations corresponding to global c variables
    * "Heap": Expands and contracts dynamically as calls to malloc() and free() are made
    * "Shared Libraries": Code for the C Standard Library (stdlib.h, math.h, etc); see Dynamic Linking (Chaper 7)
    * "Stack": top of user address space. implements function calls in C and variables
    * "Kernel Virtual Memory": reserved for kernel. "no user can read or write these contents directly, and must instead invoke the kernel to do so"

  "Concurrency and Parallelism"
  - "Concurrency" is when a system has multiple, simultaneous activities
  - "Parallelism" is applied concurrency to increase speed

    "Thread-level Concurrency"
    - L1 and L2 are per-core, with a shared L3 cache
    - Hyperthreading allows a CPU to make decisions based on availability. If task 1 needs to wait for something, it will choose to move task 2 forward

    "Instruction-Level Parallelism"
    - modern CPUs can execute multiple instructions at a time, with 2-4 instructions per clock cycle (probably higher today)
    - A processor that can handle more than 1 instruction per cycle is known as a "superscalar processor"

    "SIMD Parallelism"
    - Allows for multiple instructions at the bit level, such as adding 8 pairs of floats in parallel. See SWAR from my notes on C Programming: A Modern Approach for an example


